Narrative from partner:
4 adult male asylum seekers from Nigeria, Cameroon, Bangladesh, and Sudan were officially employed following employment counselling and job mediation support provided by CCR. The beneficiaries were employed at the following companies:

Ultra Electronics;
Serviabil SRL;
wine production company;
car wash service.

The employment support provided by CCR included counselling regarding access to the labour market, information on legal employment procedures in the Republic of Moldova, referrals to employers, and mediation between beneficiaries and companies.

A1. Provision of informational support to Ukrainian refugees through a 24/7 National Hotline service for Persons with Disabilities. 
In the 1st quarter of 2026, Keystone operators offered 24/7 informational support, received 98 calls from refugees, 68 females and 30 males (50 persons with disabilities, 22 persons with chronic illnesses, 24 persons without a disability status, 2 persons with unknown status). These numbers are on track toward the annual target of 200 callers. Most of the callers are people with disabilities or with chronic diseases. The main locations of the callers were: Anenii Noi, Bălți, Cantemir, Chișinău, Dondușeni, Glodeni, Călărași, Taraclia, Căușeni, Nisporeni, Transnistria, Telenești, Cahul and Orhei.
During January - June, beneficiaries who called on SATGPD benefited from informational support (56 calls), 39 calls were referred internally to the UNHCR mobile project team and the internal APSCF project team and 3 inter-institutional collaborations were initiated, 56 calls were provided with informational support. Persons contacted to receive information and guidance on the following topics:
•	The possibility of benefiting from a disability pension from the UA on the territory of the Republic of Moldova;
•	How to access medical services offered by a psychiatrist to obtain a prescription for medication;
•	Information on access to medical services for people with oncological conditions;
•	How to access primary and emergency medical services in the Republic of Moldova;
•	Contact details of medical consultants for refugees;
•	Contact details of organizations that provide free medications;
•	Request for medications for people with heart disease, diabetes, referral to primary care;
•	Contact details of the Red Cross to request aid in the form of food products;
•	Disability classification procedure in the Republic of Moldova;
•	Information on accessing early intervention services for children with disabilities;
•	Accessing disability allowances in the RM for children with disabilities from the UA;
•	Accessing disability allowances for children with disabilities from the UA;
•	Conditions for enrolling children with severe disabilities in the "Personal Assistance" social service;
•	Conditions for benefiting from social aid for refugees in a situation of vulnerability.
39 internal referrals were made to the UNHCR project team and to the APSCF project team, in order to facilitate beneficiaries' access to psychological support services.
Of these, 26 cases were referred to the UNHCR project mobile team. In 22 cases, beneficiaries received the necessary support, in accordance with the identified needs. For 4 people, the provision of support was not possible: 2 people had already benefited from assistance in the first quarter of 2026 and were no longer eligible for the second quarter, and for 2 people the identified needs did not fall within the types of services offered by the project. These people were referred to other organizations to benefit from the necessary assistance, including to cover medication needs.
At the same time, 13 cases were referred to the APSCF project team to benefit from specialized psychological support provided by a psychotherapist. The referred people benefited from psychological support.
3 inter-institutional collaborations were initiated to resolve the cases of people who called the service.
A2. Countrywide targeted support to persons with disabilities from refugee and local communities based on case management and needs assessment.  
During the reporting period, Keystone provided countrywide targeted support to persons with disabilities from refugee and host communities through case management and comprehensive needs assessments. This approach enabled the team to identify individual needs and ensure timely, tailored assistance for persons referred through the hotline, access to medical services, assistive devices, medical equipment, and rehabilitation support.
Medical support remained a key focus during the reporting period. In total, 156 targeted health-related services were provided to 114 individuals with disabilities, indicating that some beneficiaries received more than one service based on their assessed needs. Among the 114 individuals supported, 65 were women and girls (57.0%) and 49 were men and boys (43.0%). In terms of services delivered, women also accounted for the majority, with 91 services provided to female beneficiaries (58.3%) compared to 65 services provided to male beneficiaries (41.7%). The individuals supported included 68 Ukrainian refugees and 46 Moldovan host community members, while the services delivered included 97 services to Ukrainian beneficiaries and 59 services to Moldovan beneficiaries. Support was provided across 25 locations: Basarabeasca, Bălți, Briceni, Cantemir, Căușeni, Chișinău, Cimișlia, Criuleni, Dondușeni, Edineț, Fălești, Florești, Glodeni, Hîncești, Ialoveni, Nisporeni, Orhei, Left Bank Dniester, Rezina, Rîșcani, Sîngerei, Soroca, Ștefan Vodă, Taraclia, and Ungheni. This shows that both at individual and service-delivery levels, women and girls represented a slightly higher share of support received, while the intervention remained inclusive of both female and male beneficiaries and ensured broad geographic coverage.
To enhance mobility, health, hygiene and independence, Keystone provided targeted in-kind and service-based assistance in line with individual needs identified through case management. Hygiene items were distributed to 94 people, helping address essential daily care needs and dignity, particularly for persons with disabilities and chronic conditions. In addition, 27 people received assistive devices to improve mobility, safety, and independence in daily life, while another 27 people were provided with medical equipment to support health monitoring and the management of disability-related and chronic conditions at home. Keystone also facilitated rehabilitation services for 6 children with developmental and multiple support needs, contributing to improved functionality and access to specialized care. Furthermore, 2 people were supported with laboratory tests, enabling timely diagnosis and follow-up medical interventions. Overall, this support complemented health care assistance and strengthened beneficiaries’ ability to manage daily living, mobility, and health-related needs.
Rehabilitation for children was another important area of intervention. Six children aged 4–7 years, diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder and multiple developmental support needs, benefited from two types of rehabilitation services: ABA therapy and socio-emotional therapy with sensory integration. These interventions aimed to support children’s functional development, emotional regulation, communication, and social inclusion.
A3. Advocacy component
Keystone actively strengthened information outreach, coordination, and advocacy for disability inclusion during the reporting period. The organization continued to provide technical support on accessibility, helping ensure that essential information and referral pathways remained accessible to refugees with disabilities and older people. Keystone also shared findings from disability mapping exercises within relevant coordination forums, contributing to a better understanding of existing service gaps, barriers to access, and priority areas for further improvement. 
As co-chair of the Disability and Age Technical Group (DATF), alongside UNHCR, Keystone continued to play an active role in strengthening coordination, knowledge exchange, joint problem-solving, and strategic advocacy on disability inclusion among humanitarian actors, authorities, and service providers. During the reporting period, Keystone contributed to the 2025–2026 Refugee Response Plan Year-End/Mid-Term Report, updated the group’s membership and contact lists, and submitted revised Terms of Reference and Work Plans for DATF. These efforts helped reinforce the group’s coordination structure, clarify priorities, and support more coherent joint action among partners working with refugees with disabilities and older persons.
Additionally, three DATF meetings were organized, two in April and one in June. These meetings supported dialogue on disability inclusion, access to services, and referral mechanisms for refugees and host community members with disabilities. The 3rd DATF meeting placed particular emphasis on a question-and-answer session with representatives of the National Health Insurance Company and the Community Mental Health Center, who provided clarifications on access to health insurance, available services, eligibility procedures, and referral pathways. This exchange helped strengthen participants’ knowledge and improve the quality of referrals for persons with disabilities and older refugees requiring support. 
Keystone continued to provide technical assistance to humanitarian actors, contributing to improved accessibility of refugee support services and referral pathways. In March, the organization presented the findings of its disability mapping exercise at the Protection Working Group (PWG), supporting a more evidence-based understanding of existing service gaps, accessibility barriers, and priority needs of refugees with disabilities and older persons. These discussions helped coordination actors better align interventions, strengthen referrals, and prioritize support for persons with disabilities within the broader protection response.
On 22 April, Keystone organized a round table discussion with public authorities and strategic partners to address key challenges related to disability inclusion and access to essential services for refugees with disabilities and older persons. The event provided an important platform for dialogue between institutional actors, humanitarian organizations, and service providers. The discussion helped strengthen cooperation among key stakeholders and generated practical feedback on existing gaps, institutional responsibilities, and opportunities to improve coordination. Based on the inputs and recommendations collected during the round table, Keystone developed a policy paper aimed at documenting the main systemic barriers affecting refugees with disabilities and older persons, promoting evidence-based advocacy, and proposing concrete measures to strengthen inclusive service delivery and referral pathways. The policy paper also seeks to support dialogue with national authorities and partners on improving access to health care, disability determination procedures, social protection, and community-based support services for persons with disabilities within the refugee response.
To build robust referral pathways and coordination mechanisms, Keystone conducted comprehensive mapping of service providers supporting both refugees with disabilities and older adults. This effort was designed to ensure that beneficiaries could access the most appropriate support and resources available to them.
To strengthen participatory approaches, Keystone organized two focus group discussions with refugees with disabilities in Chișinău, held in April and June. These sessions explored critical topics such as legal stay status (including planning for the period after Temporary Protection) and challenges related to inclusion and integration. The feedback gathered informed ongoing program development and helped tailor support to the specific needs of the community.
As part of its protection work, Keystone reached out to 400 beneficiaries to provide information on the extension of Temporary Protection and other forms of protection, while identifying individuals requiring legal assistance or mobile support. An additional 20 beneficiaries were contacted and informed about summer activities available for children with disabilities and their parents. These outreach efforts ensured that vulnerable individuals received timely information and were connected to relevant resources, reinforcing Keystone’s commitment to comprehensive and responsive service delivery.
